<HelpHead Page="how-to-play/mahjong-cube" Title="How to play Mahjong Cube"
          Description="How to play Mahjong Cube: take matching pairs from a cube of mahjong tiles you can turn. Which tiles can be taken, the controls, scoring and tips." />

<article class="help" lang="en">
    <h1>How to play Mahjong Cube</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        Mahjong Cube takes the matching game into three dimensions. The tiles form a solid cube instead of a flat pile, and you
        turn the cube in your hands to find pairs on every side.
    </p>

    <figure>
        <div class="board-picture"><img src="help-pictures/mahjong-cube.png" alt="A Mahjong Cube game: a small cube of ivory tiles, four tiles along each edge" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" /></div>
        <figcaption>The small cube: four tiles along each edge, 64 tiles in all.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>The goal</h2>
    <p>Take the whole cube apart in matching pairs. Every tile has to go.</p>

    <h2>Which tiles can be taken</h2>
    <p>
        Each tile in the cube is a small cube itself, with six sides. A tile can be taken when <strong>two of its sides that meet at
        an edge are open</strong>: one open side across (left or right) and one open side on another axis (above or below, in front
        or behind). Two open sides facing opposite ways, like left and right, aren't enough.
    </p>
    <RuleDiagram Kind="cube" Caption="On a full cube, the corners and edges can be taken first. Tiles in the middle of a face have only one open side until their neighbours go." />
    <p>
        Tiles that can't be taken are drawn slightly dimmed, so the ones you can take stand out. As pairs come away, the tiles
        underneath open up and the cube gets smaller, layer by layer.
    </p>

    <h2>The tiles</h2>
    <p>
        The cube uses the same tiles as the other games. Each tile matches only its identical twin. The faces are printed on every
        side of each tile, so a tile reads the same whichever way the cube is turned.
    </p>
    <TileGuide />

    <h2>Playing on Mahjong Haus</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Choose Mahjong Cube</strong> in the settings (the gear in the top bar), and a size: small (4×4×4, 64 tiles) or
            large (6×6×6, 216 tiles).</li>
        <li><strong>Choose a stone and a face</strong> on the Stone and Face card, and a background.</li>
        <li><strong>Turn the cube.</strong> Drag to turn it. Pinch, or use the mouse wheel, to zoom.</li>
        <li><strong>Take a pair.</strong> Tap a tile you can take, then its twin, which may be on another side: turn the cube to
            find it.</li>
        <li><strong>Use the tools under the cube:</strong> undo and redo, a hint, shuffle, the demo, and pause.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Scoring</h2>
    <p>
        The cube is scored like the other games: points for every tile, pace and speed bonuses, finish bonuses for a quick finish,
        and a no-shuffle bonus. Each size has its own leaderboard. <a href="how-to-play/mahjong-solitaire#scoring">See how scoring works.</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Tips</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Turn the cube often.</strong> A tile's twin is just as likely to be on the far side. Take a full turn around
            before deciding you're stuck.</li>
        <li><strong>Work from the corners in.</strong> Corners and edges open first. Taking them evenly, rather than digging a hole
            in one face, keeps more tiles available.</li>
        <li><strong>Don't strand the last of a kind.</strong> Before taking a pair, check where the other two tiles of that kind
            are. If one sits deep in the middle, keep a way open to it.</li>
        <li><strong>Start small.</strong> The small cube is quick to learn on; the large one hides 64 tiles inside it, out of sight until the outer layers come away.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <dl>
        <dt>Why can't I take a tile with three open sides in a row?</dt>
        <dd>Open sides only count in pairs that meet at an edge. Left and right are on the same axis, so they count as one; you
            need one of those plus an open side above, below, in front or behind.</dd>
        <dt>Can every cube be cleared?</dt>
        <dd>With Guaranteed winnable path on, yes: the cube is dealt so there's a way through, though a wrong order can still leave
            you stuck. Undo, or shuffle the tiles left.</dd>
        <dt>Is there a quick way to see the rules while playing?</dt>
        <dd>The first time you play, a short note explains how to turn the cube and which tiles can be taken. The ⓘ next to the game
            in the settings shows the rule too.</dd>
    </dl>

